I'll solve this directly via thinking. Let me work through the geometry.

**Setup.** Let $\Gamma=\mathcal C$. Let $\omega_A=(BOC),\omega_B=(COA),\omega_C=(AOB)$ be the three circumcircles; all four circles $\Gamma,\omega_A,\omega_B,\omega_C$ pass through $O$.

**Radical-axis identification of the six lines through $O$.** Since any two of these circles share $O$, their radical axis is a line through $O$, and it is exactly the line joining $O$ to their other common point:

- $\operatorname{rad}(\omega_B,\omega_C)=OA$ (they share $O$ and $A$) $\Rightarrow$ second intersection with $\Gamma$ is $P$.
- $\operatorname{rad}(\omega_C,\omega_A)=OB$ $\Rightarrow$ $Q$.
- $\operatorname{rad}(\omega_A,\omega_B)=OC$ $\Rightarrow$ $R$.
- $\operatorname{rad}(\Gamma,\omega_A)=OK$ $\Rightarrow$ $K$.
- $\operatorname{rad}(\Gamma,\omega_B)=OL$ $\Rightarrow$ $L$.
- $\operatorname{rad}(\Gamma,\omega_C)=OM$ $\Rightarrow$ $M$.

**The coefficient plane.** Put $O$ at the origin. Every circle through $O$ has equation $|X|^2-w\cdot X=0$ for a unique vector $w$ (its "coefficient"). Write $w_\Gamma,w_A,w_B,w_C$ for $\Gamma,\omega_A,\omega_B,\omega_C$, and regard these as four points $W_\Gamma,W_A,W_B,W_C$ in an auxiliary plane (the $w$-plane). The radical axis of the circles with coefficients $w_i,w_j$ is $(w_j-w_i)\cdot X=0$: a line through $O$ whose **direction is perpendicular to $\overrightarrow{W_iW_j}$**. Hence the six lines $OA,OB,OC,OK,OL,OM$ have directions respectively perpendicular to the six sides
$$W_BW_C,\; W_CW_A,\; W_AW_B,\; W_\Gamma W_A,\; W_\Gamma W_B,\; W_\Gamma W_C$$
of the complete quadrangle $W_\Gamma W_AW_BW_C$.

**Desargues involution theorem on the line at infinity.** Let $\ell_\infty$ be the line at infinity of the $w$-plane. The six sides of the complete quadrangle $W_\Gamma W_AW_BW_C$ meet $\ell_\infty$ in six points (their directions). By the Desargues involution theorem, these six points form three pairs of an involution on $\ell_\infty$, the pairing being **opposite sides**:
$$(W_BW_C,\,W_\Gamma W_A),\quad (W_CW_A,\,W_\Gamma W_B),\quad (W_AW_B,\,W_\Gamma W_C).$$

**Transfer to $\Gamma$.** The map "direction $\mapsto$ line through $O$ with that direction" identifies $\ell_\infty$ with the pencil of lines through $O$; composing with the $90^\circ$ rotation (a projectivity of $\ell_\infty$) and then with the map
$$\varphi:\{\text{lines through }O\}\longrightarrow\Gamma,\qquad \ell\mapsto\text{second intersection of }\ell\text{ with }\Gamma,$$
gives a projectivity $\ell_\infty\to\Gamma$. (The map $\varphi$ is a perspectivity from $O$, hence a projectivity.) Projectivities carry involutions to involutions, so the above involution on $\ell_\infty$ transports to an involution $\sigma$ on $\Gamma$ whose three pairs are exactly
$$(P,K),\quad (Q,L),\quad (R,M),$$
because $P,K$ correspond to the opposite sides $W_BW_C,W_\Gamma W_A$, etc.

**Involutions on a conic give concurrent chords.** It remains to use the standard fact: *the chords joining the pairs of an involution on a conic are concurrent.* 

Indeed, take two pairs $(P,K)$ and $(Q,L)$; let $S=PK\cap QL$. Projection from $S$ defines an involution $\tau$ on $\Gamma$ (each line through $S$ meets $\Gamma$ in a conjugate pair). This $\tau$ agrees with $\sigma$ on the two pairs $(P,K),(Q,L)$; an involution on a rational curve is determined by two pairs, so $\tau=\sigma$. Therefore the third pair $(R,M)$ also lies on a line through $S$, i.e. $RM$ passes through $S$.

Thus $PK,QL,RM$ meet at the single point $S$. $\blacksquare$

